course: ai
lesson: 1
title: What is AI? Pattern-guessers, not people
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: AI, short for artificial intelligence (artificial means made by people, not grown by nature), is the name for computer programs that learn patterns from huge piles of examples instead of following only rules a person wrote line by line.
explain: Think of a jar of a million sweets: after tasting enough of them you would guess that red means strawberry and green means lime. AI "tasted" millions of sentences or pictures in the same way and learned what usually goes together.
explain: When your phone suggests "time" after you type "Once upon a", it is not reading your mind; it is betting on the most usual next word.
explain: A chatbot that says "good morning" sounds friendly, but there is nobody inside it who woke up, ate breakfast or will miss you at school.
explain: So AI is a very fast, very well-read pattern-guesser: brilliant at the usual, shaky at the unusual, and not a person.
fact: AI programs learn patterns from very large numbers of examples, such as sentences or photos.
fact: A chatbot's friendly greeting is the kind of reply its training makes likely; it does not mean someone is alive inside it.
fact: Phone keyboards and chatbots predict which words are most likely to come next.
fact: A chatbot has no body, so it cannot taste chocolate, smell flowers or feel tired.
fact: Nobody typed the chatbot's answers in one by one; each answer is produced fresh when you ask.
fact: An AI that learned what cats look like can be fooled by something fluffy and cat-shaped, such as a slipper.
fact: When a chatbot describes its weekend or its picnic, it is making up a story, because it has no weekends.
fact: Small AI programs hide in everyday things, such as phone cameras that sharpen photos and blur backgrounds.
fact: AI in films, with robots that make wicked plans of their own, is invented to be exciting; real AI is programs built and run by people.
fact: A robot vacuum cleaner uses sensors and a map to do one job; it does not understand the world the way a dog does.
fact: AI is good at the usual cases it has seen many times and often weak at unusual or brand-new ones.
exercise: Sam's chatbot said "good morning" to him. What does that show? | It was built to answer in friendly sentences | It woke up this morning and was glad to see him | Someone at the company typed it in for him personally | It has been waiting for him all night
exercise: Mia's cousin compares AI to tasting a million sweets. What is she explaining? | AI learns what usually goes together from many examples | AI eats information the way people eat their food at dinner | AI needs sugar to make its computers run faster | AI only works on questions about sweets
exercise: Jake types "Once upon a" and the phone suggests "time". Why? | "Time" is the word that most often comes next | The phone heard Jake thinking the word in his head | Jake typed the word "time" by accident earlier | The phone has a list of every story ever written
exercise: Lily asks the chatbot if it likes chocolate. What is the honest answer? | It cannot taste anything, because it has no body | It loves milk chocolate much more than dark chocolate | It only likes chocolate on special days | It tasted chocolate once while it was being built
exercise: Connor says AI is a computer brain that will take over, like in the films. What is closer to the truth? | Today's AI is programs that find patterns, built and run by people | Real AI already makes its own secret plans to take over the world | Film robots are copied exactly from real AI | AI is a living brain kept inside a computer
exercise: The cat app calls a fluffy slipper "cat". What does this mistake show? | It learned from examples and can be fooled by look-alikes | It knows the slipper belongs to a cat, so it named the cat | The slipper really was a sleeping cat | The app is broken and must be thrown away at once
exercise: Grandad asks who typed all the answers into the chatbot. What happened really? | Nobody did; it learned patterns and makes each answer fresh | A big team of people sits in an office and writes every answer by hand | The answers were copied from one big encyclopedia | Each user types answers for the next user
exercise: Maddie's bot tells her about its lovely weekend picnic. What is going on? | It is making up a story that fits the question | It really went to the park with its family on Sunday | It is remembering a family picnic from last summer | It is describing another user's weekend at the park
exercise: Mum says the phone camera uses AI. What might that AI do? | Sharpen the photo and smooth or blur parts of it | Decide who is allowed to be in the photo and who is not | Send each photo to a person who fixes it | Take photos on its own when nobody is looking
exercise: In the "Be the AI" game Lily guesses a missing word from a pile of sentences. What part of AI is she copying? | Learning patterns from examples to guess what fits | Typing very fast on a big keyboard | Remembering every sentence word for word, forever | Asking a friend for the right answer
exercise: Jake says the robot hoover is smarter than the dog. Fair? | No, it is good at one job; the dog understands far more | Yes, it can do everything a dog can do, and it never needs a walk | Yes, because it knows the whole house by heart | No, robot hoovers have no computer inside
exercise: What does the "A" in AI stand for? | Artificial, meaning made by people | Animal, because it learns like pets do | Automatic, because it never needs power | Alive, because it can talk back to you
exercise: Where is AI usually good, and where is it shaky? | Good at usual cases, shaky at unusual ones | Good at unusual cases, shaky at usual ones | Equally perfect at every kind of case | Only good when the question is about films
exercise: Which of these is a real everyday use of AI? | A phone keyboard suggesting your next word | A kettle that boils when you switch it on at the wall | A torch that shines when you press the button | A bike bell that rings when you push the lever
test: "mate my chatbot's alive, it said morning and asked how i slept" What do you tell him? | Friendly words are what it learned to say, nobody's in there | He's right, it must've been awake all night waiting to chat to him | Only alive bots ask about sleep, so yes | It's alive only in the mornings
test: What would happen if Jake typed "Once upon a banana"? | The phone would probably guess badly, because that is unusual | The phone would know exactly what comes next, because it knows every story | The phone would refuse to work | The phone would fix it to "Once upon a time" forever
test: In a cartoon, a chatbot rubs its tummy and says it's starving for chips. What does the cartoon get wrong? | A chatbot has no body, so it can't be hungry | Chatbots prefer sweets to chips, so it would ask for sweets | Chatbots only get hungry at night | Nothing, chatbots eat electricity like chips
test: "if nobody typed the answer how come it's right most of the time??" Best reply? | It learned from huge amounts of text how answers usually go | Somebody secretly checks every answer first before you see it | It is never right, it only looks right | It copies each answer from one website
test: Chloe's teacher holds up a ginger fluffy cushion and the app says "cat". What should the class learn? | The app learned a look, not what a cat really is | The cushion must contain cat hair | The app is cleverer than the class | Cushions and cats are the same to everyone
test: Gran thinks AI is "a little man in the phone". What is the truth? | It is a program finding patterns, with no little man | A small robot lives inside every phone and talks back to whoever asks | A worker listens and answers from far away | It is a recording of one clever person's voice
test: "AI's gonna take over the world, it's in every film innit" Which answer fits real AI? | Films make up robot villains; real AI is tools that people build and run | It already runs every country in secret, the films just show it | Films are documentaries about real AI | It will happen next week, the films said so
test: Which is the best way to test whether the phone is "reading your mind"? | Type something surprising and see it guess wrong | Think very hard about a word and watch the screen | Ask the phone politely whether it can read your mind | Turn the phone off and on again to reset its guesses
test: Maddie asks, "Did that picnic really happen to you?" What would an honest bot say? | No, I don't have a life; I made up a story | Yes, it was last Saturday in the park | I can't remember, it was a long time ago | Yes, but it rained in the afternoon
test: Sam's camera made his face look smooth in a photo. What should he remember? | Photos can be quietly changed, so real faces don't look like that | His face really is that smooth in real life, the camera just shows it | The camera can see things eyes cannot | Smoothed photos are always more truthful
test: The robot hoover keeps bumping into the same chair leg. What does that show? | It follows sensors and simple rules, not real understanding | It is angry with the chair and keeps trying to knock it over on purpose | It wants to move the chair out of the way | It is trying to clean the chair leg
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises contemporary artificial intelligence? | Computer programs that derive patterns from very large quantities of examples | Living organisms of electronic construction possessing their own secret intentions | Recordings of answers composed in advance by human operators | Mechanical devices that comprehend the world as animals do
exam: It is given that a keyboard proposes the word "time" subsequent to the phrase "Once upon a". Identify the principal reason for this. | The word in question most frequently follows the phrase in written text | The device has perceived the thoughts of its user | The word has been stored in respect of that user alone | A remote operator has supplied the word
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a chatbot that recounts its activities at the weekend. | The account is invented, the program possessing no personal life | The account is a faithful recollection of real events at the weekend | The account describes the activities of its designers | The account is drawn from the memory of a previous user
exam: An image classifier is observed to designate a fluffy slipper as a cat. Ascertain the inference which may properly be drawn. | The classifier has learned visual resemblance from examples and may be misled by similar objects | The classifier possesses knowledge that the slipper belongs to a cat | The classifier is functioning without error, slippers being a variety of cat | The classifier has been deliberately instructed to deceive its users
